## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit reasoning, non-branching logic, and careful boundary handling across hi/lo halves. The value is a power of two when exactly one bit in the 128-bit space is set.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value split into hi/lo; treat as two 64-bit lanes.
- Power-of-two check: a nonzero 64-bit word is a power of two if (x & (x-1)) == 0.
- Branchless composition: the single 1 must reside either in lo (and hi == 0) or in hi (and lo == 0).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch in C-like syntax (language-agnostic semantics)
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  unsigned int loCase = (lo != 0) && ((lo & (lo - 1)) == 0) && (hi == 0);
  unsigned int hiCase = (hi != 0) && ((hi & (hi - 1)) == 0) && (lo == 0);
  return (loCase || hiCase) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to an embedded environment with no 64-bit ops available?
- How would you verify correctness across all 128-bit values efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-hacking techniques across a 128-bit value by splitting into two 64-bit halves and summing, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit popcount via parallel bit counting masks
- Treat 128-bit as two 64-bit blocks
- No loops or branches; purely bitwise ops

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7f;
}
unsigned int countBits128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage __builtin_popcountll if available?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

