- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about 128-bit values and apply bit-twiddling without branching. This requires cross-half logic and edge-case handling.

Key Concepts
- 128-bit decomposition into hi and lo
- a 64-bit value is a power of two if v & (v-1) == 0 and v != 0
- exactly one half nonzero implies overall power-of-two only if that half is itself a power of two

Code Example

```c
uint8_t isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  // Exactly one half must be nonzero and that half must be a power of two
  int hiPow = (hi & (hi - 1)) == 0 && hi != 0;
  int loPow = (lo & (lo - 1)) == 0 && lo != 0;
  return ((hi == 0) ^ (lo == 0)) ? (hi ? hiPow : loPow) : 0;
}
```

