## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit values without hardware helpers.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation as hi/lo
- Leading-zero counting
- Branching vs branchless approaches
- Correct handling of zero input

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo helper assuming a 64-bit lz routine exists as lz64
static int lz64(uint64_t v){
  int n = 0;
  if ((v & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) == 0) { n += 32; v <<= 32; }
  if ((v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 16; v <<= 16; }
  if ((v & 0xFF00000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 8; v <<= 8; }
  if ((v & 0xF000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 4; v <<= 4; }
  if ((v & 0xC000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 2; v <<= 2; }
  if ((v & 0x8000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 1; }
  return n;
}
int countLeadingZeros128(uint128 x){
  if (x.hi != 0) return lz64(x.hi);
  if (x.lo != 0) return 64 + lz64(x.lo);
  return 128;
}
```

